# easyNode Esplora front — Caddy configuration template.
#
# Ported from the deployment that serves api.btxscan.io today, and verified
# against it on 2026-09-06 (PROVENANCE.md). The comments are the valuable part:
# each one records an incident, and they are kept verbatim where the reasoning
# still applies. What was NOT ported is listed at the bottom.
#
# ⚠ This needs a Caddy built with the rate-limit plugin
#   (github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit). Stock Caddy refuses this file with
#   "unrecognized directive: rate_limit". build-caddy.sh builds the right one.
#
# Environment, read when the Caddyfile is adapted:
#     BTX_ESPLORA_HOST   the site address. A hostname gets automatic HTTPS
#                        (esplora-1.example.com); http://127.0.0.1:3080 serves
#                        plain HTTP on localhost, which is what the easyNode
#                        app starts with until an operator puts a name on it.
#     BTX_ESPLORA_RUN    where the freshness guardian writes its markers.
#                        Default /run (the systemd deployment); the app uses
#                        <datadir>/esplora/run because it does not run as root.
#     BTX_ESPLORA_ELECTRS   the electrs REST address. Default 127.0.0.1:3000.
#     BTX_ESPLORA_BTXD_RPC  btxd's REST+RPC address. Default 127.0.0.1:19334.
#
#     BTX_ESPLORA_HOST=esplora-1.example.com caddy run --config Caddyfile.template --adapter caddyfile
#
# The last two default to exactly the addresses this file used to hardcode, so
# a deployment that sets neither behaves as before. They exist because a
# hardcoded upstream cannot be tested: deploy/esplora/test-front.sh runs this
# very file against stubs on free ports, and before that this configuration had
# never been started at all, let alone asked what header it emits. They also
# let a second instance run beside a live one.
#
# Assumes:
#     electrs  on $BTX_ESPLORA_ELECTRS  (see electrs.service.template)
#     btxd RPC on $BTX_ESPLORA_BTXD_RPC
#     the staleness guardian writing $BTX_ESPLORA_RUN/btx-{fresh,stale,unverified}
#
# ⚠ Do not enable this on a pruned node. electrs indexes from block files on
#   disk and a pruned datadir deletes them; easyNode refuses the mode outright
#   (crates/btx-core/src/esplora.rs) and this config would only fail later and
#   more confusingly. See docs/esplora-mode.md.
{
	# No admin endpoint. Caddy otherwise opens an UNAUTHENTICATED control API
	# on 127.0.0.1:2019 that can rewrite its whole configuration, which is a
	# fourth listening port this file never declared and nothing here uses. It
	# is also a startup failure waiting to happen: Caddy REFUSES TO START when
	# 2019 is taken, and something else holding it is not hypothetical — on the
	# machine this was developed on, a Windows process had it and WSL2 shares
	# localhost.
	#
	# Set BTX_ESPLORA_ADMIN to an address if you want it back.
	admin {$BTX_ESPLORA_ADMIN:off}

	# BEFORE `handle`, and this is load-bearing. The original read
	# `order rate_limit before reverse_proxy`, which worked there because its
	# rate_limit sat inside a `route` block. This port dropped that wrapper
	# along with the home-IP exemption it existed for, leaving a bare
	# `rate_limit` beside the `handle` blocks — and `handle` sorts BEFORE
	# `reverse_proxy`, so the limiter was ordered after a directive that
	# terminates routing and never ran at all.
	#
	# It failed silently and looked completely fine: every request was served,
	# every header was correct, and the endpoint had no rate limit. Measured by
	# deploy/esplora/test-front.sh, which is the only reason this is not still
	# true — a 300-request burst returned 300 successes before, and 116 of them
	# are 429 now.
	#
	# The ordering is a total order, so `before handle` also keeps rate_limit
	# ahead of `reverse_proxy` inside the @rawblock handler below.
	order rate_limit before handle
}

# Shared Esplora API config. Caddy is the SINGLE source of CORS; upstream
# (electrs --cors) CORS headers are stripped so responses never carry a
# duplicate Access-Control-Allow-Origin — browsers reject duplicates outright,
# and that broke the web wallet once already.
(esplora_api) {
	encode gzip zstd
	@options method OPTIONS
	handle @options {
		header Access-Control-Allow-Origin "*"
		header Access-Control-Allow-Methods "GET, POST, OPTIONS"
		header Access-Control-Allow-Headers "Content-Type"
		respond 204
	}
	header Access-Control-Allow-Origin "*"
	header Access-Control-Allow-Methods "GET, POST, OPTIONS"
	header -Server
	header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"

	# ── Full block serialization over HTTP ───────────────────────────────────
	# Requested by an operator migrating a fleet onto the canonical branch,
	# whose P2P bulk sync ran at 1-2 blocks/min because MATMUL_CONSENSUS peers
	# advertise blocks but report synced_blocks=-1 and serve nothing.
	#
	# Esplora's /block/<hash>/raw is NOT usable for submitblock. Measured on
	# block 189000: btxd serialises 383 bytes, esplora returns 381 — identical
	# for the first 381 and then TRUNCATED by 2. The MatMul proof is not
	# stripped (that diagnosis was wrong); the truncation breaks
	# deserialisation, which is why submitblock produced an invalid chain.
	#
	# btxd's own REST interface returns the complete serialization, so exactly
	# that one read-only path is exposed and nothing else.
	#
	# SECURITY: btxd serves REST on the SAME port as JSON-RPC (19334). Only GET
	# requests whose path starts with /rest/block/ are forwarded. RPC is POST to
	# "/" and can never match this matcher, so the node's RPC stays unreachable
	# from the internet. Rate limited more tightly than the Esplora API, since
	# each hit costs a disk read.
	@rawblock {
		method GET
		path /rest/block/*
	}
	handle @rawblock {
		rate_limit {
			zone rawblock {
				key {remote_host}
				events 60
				window 10s
			}
		}
		header X-Btx-Source "btxd-rest"
		reverse_proxy {$BTX_ESPLORA_BTXD_RPC:127.0.0.1:19334} {
			transport http {
				read_timeout 30s
				write_timeout 30s
			}
		}
	}

	rate_limit {
		zone perip {
			key {remote_host}
			events 200
			window 10s
		}
	}
	request_body {
		max_size 30MB
	}

	# ── Freshness-gated, never chain-swapped ─────────────────────────────────
	# The guardian writes exactly one of $BTX_ESPLORA_RUN/btx-{fresh,stale,
	# unverified} and Caddy matches on its presence.
	#
	# This exists because a node once stopped following the chain at block
	# 184,999 and kept answering 200 with stale data. Error-driven failover
	# cannot see that: a stalled node is not an error, it is a confident wrong
	# answer.
	#
	# ⚠ WE DO NOT FAIL OVER TO ANOTHER CHAIN, and both former fallbacks are
	# object lessons in why:
	#
	#   - explorer.minebtx.com has been HTTP 503 since the fork, so proxying to
	#     it turned this API into a 503 generator. Its use as the staleness
	#     REFERENCE was worse: a dead reference silently CLEARED the marker, and
	#     that accident is the only reason the endpoint kept serving at all.
	#
	#   - esplora.btxbyronbay.com follows the heavier UNATTESTED branch —
	#     identical through 187,660, split at 187,661 (attested ad62b638…,
	#     byronbay 2d85ef53…) — and its UTXO index under-reports spends in the
	#     COMMON range, on one address listing 35 outputs as unspent that a good
	#     node proves spent, overstating that balance by ~4,042 BTX.
	#
	# An overstated balance reaching a signing wallet is worse than a stale one.
	# So always serve your own node and TELL the caller how fresh it is.
	#
	# X-Btx-Freshness: fresh | stale | unverified
	#   unverified = no witness was reachable, we do not know, and we refuse to
	#   claim we are current.
	#
	# ONE DELIBERATE CHANGE FROM THE ORIGINAL (2026-09-06): with NO marker
	# present this front answers `unverified`, not `fresh`. The original could
	# default to `fresh` because its guardian always writes a marker; here the
	# front is also started by the easyNode app, and a front that says `fresh`
	# before anything has checked is the exact confident wrong answer the
	# guardian exists to prevent. `fresh` now needs its marker like the others.
	@stale file {
		root "{$BTX_ESPLORA_RUN:/run}"
		try_files btx-stale
	}
	handle @stale {
		header X-Btx-Upstream "local"
		header X-Btx-Freshness "stale"
		reverse_proxy {$BTX_ESPLORA_ELECTRS:127.0.0.1:3000} {
			header_down -Access-Control-Allow-Origin
			header_down -Access-Control-Allow-Methods
			header_down -Access-Control-Allow-Headers
			transport http {
				read_timeout 30s
				write_timeout 30s
			}
		}
	}
	@fresh file {
		# QUOTED: the app puts this directory under the datadir, and a datadir
		# path containing a space is ordinary on macOS and Windows. Unquoted,
		# Caddy reads the second word as another argument and refuses the whole
		# configuration — the front would not start, for a reason nowhere near
		# the freshness markers it is about.
		root "{$BTX_ESPLORA_RUN:/run}"
		try_files btx-fresh
	}
	handle @fresh {
		header X-Btx-Upstream "local"
		header X-Btx-Freshness "fresh"
		reverse_proxy {$BTX_ESPLORA_ELECTRS:127.0.0.1:3000} {
			header_down -Access-Control-Allow-Origin
			header_down -Access-Control-Allow-Methods
			header_down -Access-Control-Allow-Headers
			transport http {
				read_timeout 30s
				write_timeout 30s
			}
		}
	}
	handle {
		header X-Btx-Upstream "local"
		header X-Btx-Freshness "unverified"
		reverse_proxy {$BTX_ESPLORA_ELECTRS:127.0.0.1:3000} {
			header_down -Access-Control-Allow-Origin
			header_down -Access-Control-Allow-Methods
			header_down -Access-Control-Allow-Headers
			transport http {
				read_timeout 30s
				write_timeout 30s
			}
		}
	}
}

{$BTX_ESPLORA_HOST} {
	import esplora_api
}

# ── DELIBERATELY NOT PORTED FROM THE ORIGINAL ────────────────────────────────
#
#   - A `basic_auth` block for a private stats host. It carried a bcrypt
#     credential, which has no business in a public repository even hashed.
#   - A second site serving an unrelated internal service.
#   - A rate-limit exemption for one fixed home IP, added for a specific event
#     and marked "REMOVE THIS AFTER THE EVENT" in the original.
#   - The two concrete hostnames of the existing deployment.
#   - Request logging. The original logs ONLY wallet-User-Agent requests and
#     strips remote_ip, remote_port and Cookie — anonymous by construction. It
#     is worth copying if you want usage metrics, and worth copying EXACTLY,
#     because the filter is what makes it anonymous. It is omitted here so that
#     nobody inherits logging they did not ask for.
